#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/id #uuid "3e36e916-16af-55eb-9ece-026b68eb93ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3338" :question/problem "Consider a curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$. Find the radius of curvature of $\\mathbf r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ is given by\n$T(s)|=-\\sin (\\frac{s}{3})i + \\cos (\\frac{s}{3})j$." :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(s) &= \\frac{d}{ds}(-\\sin (\\frac{s}{3}))i + \\frac{d}{ds}(\\cos (\\frac{s}{3}))j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3}\\cos (\\frac{s}{3})i - \\frac{1}{3}\\sin (\\frac{s}{3})j \\\\\n‖T^{′}(s)‖ &= \\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cos (\\frac{s}{3}))^{2} + (-\\frac{1}{3}\\sin (\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}(\\cos^{2} (\\frac{s}{3}) + \\sin^{2} (\\frac{s}{3}))} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "7f4861fe-a783-5874-a22b-b4644257ae0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3339" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at the point $\\mathbf{r}(0)$, if the curve and the derivative $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n$\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t, \\:\\dfrac{t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf T'(t)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{(t^2+1)^3}}\\left\\langle -t, \\: 1\\right\\rangle \\,$." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ and the derivative $\\mathbf T'(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula to compute the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\mathbf \\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, $\\mathbf r'(0)$, and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨1, t⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(0) &= ⟨1, 0⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 0^{2}} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate $\\mathbf T'(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(0) &= ⟨ - (0)/(\\sqrt{(1 + 0^{2})^{3}}), (1)/(\\sqrt{(1 + 0^{2})^{3}})⟩ = ⟨0, 1⟩ \\\\\n‖T^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + 1^{2}} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(0) &= (‖r^{′}(0)‖)/(‖T^{′}(0)‖) = \\frac{1}{1} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t = 0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(0) &= (T^{′}(0))/(‖T^{′}(0)‖) = ⟨0, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) + R(0)N(0) &=  \\\\\n⟨0, 0⟩ + 1 \\cdot ⟨0, 1⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1⟩ \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1834/Source/Images/7448afe0b58173006462d2b229836749.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "8d58f131-7260-5346-9a88-60862c0a1b55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3340" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle e^{t}, \\: e^{-t} \\right\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$, $\\mathbf r'(0)$, and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}), \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{-t})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{t},-e^{-t}⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(0) &= ⟨e^{0},-e^{-0}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{(1)^{2} + (-1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}), \\frac{d}{dt}(-e^{-t})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{t}, - (-e^{-t})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{t}, e^{-t}⟩ \\\\\nr^{″}(0) &= ⟨e^{0}, e^{-0}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(0)$ and $\\mathbf r''(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(0) \\cdot r^{″}(0) &= ⟨1,-1, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨1, 1, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}0 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ = 1\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 1k \\\\\n&= 2k \\\\\n‖r^{′}(0) \\cdot r^{″}(0)‖ &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(0) &= (‖r^{′}(0)‖^{3})/(‖r^{′}(0) \\cdot r^{″}(0)‖) = ((\\sqrt{2})^{3})/(2) = \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81b0d034-c839-50d9-943f-bbbce1e02764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118342} {:answer/id #uuid 